<section
  id='case-semester-project-1-logotype'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Gemenskapet Science Museum — Logo Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Graphic design case study for a playful science museum identity created for older children, young teenagers, families, and schools.'
  data-case='semester-project-1'
  aria-labelledby='case-semester-project-1-logotype-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-semester-project-1-logotype-title'>
          Gemenskapet Science Museum
        </h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A curiosity-led museum identity that makes science feel approachable while maintaining the
          credibility of an educational organisation.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand identity and logo design</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Semester Project 1</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Audience</dt>
            <dd>Older children and young teenagers</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Adobe Illustrator</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Web and print logo suite</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='semester-project-1-logotype'></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Gemenskapet Science Museum needed an identity that encouraged young visitors to explore
        while still feeling trustworthy to families and schools. The logo had to communicate science
        and discovery quickly, remain legible across web and print, and adapt from a complete
        bilingual lockup to a small standalone icon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The museum covered several areas of science, so using only one subject as the central
            symbol would have felt too narrow. Combining too many references, however, could easily
            make the logo crowded and difficult to recognise. The main challenge was creating
            something playful enough for a younger audience without losing the clarity expected from
            an educational institution.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Represent curiosity and discovery rather than one scientific field
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep several small symbols understandable when the logo is reduced
            </li>
            <li>
              Balance a colourful, youthful icon with a clear and credible wordmark
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/logosuite-gemenskapet-science-museum-900.webp'
            data-src='logosuite-gemenskapet-science-museum-900.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='900'
            height='638'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Gemenskapet Science Museum logo suite showing primary, secondary, icon, vertical, monochrome, and reversed versions'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Logo variations created for wide, compact, monochrome, and reversed applications
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I used a magnifying glass as the main symbol because it connects observation,
              investigation, and the curiosity behind scientific learning.
            </li>
            <li>
              I divided the lens into coloured sections containing molecule, atom, and network
              symbols to suggest several areas of science without choosing only one.
            </li>
            <li>
              The rounded icon and bright colours make the identity approachable, while the clean
              sans-serif wordmark gives it a more stable and credible foundation.
            </li>
            <li>
              I added the handwritten Swedish tagline “utforska tillsammans :)” as a warmer, more
              informal layer that can be removed when space is limited.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/icon-gemenskapet-science-museum-600.webp'
            data-src='icon-gemenskapet-science-museum-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='435'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Standalone Gemenskapet Science Museum magnifying-glass icon with science symbols and Swedish tagline'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Standalone magnifying-glass icon for smaller and more compact placements
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The completed identity feels youthful and inviting without losing its educational character.
        The full logo introduces the museum clearly, while the magnifying-glass symbol can represent
        it in smaller spaces. Creating horizontal, vertical, monochrome, and reversed versions from
        the beginning also made the identity practical for both digital and printed applications.
        This project helped me understand how several visual ideas can be combined into one flexible
        symbol without making it unnecessarily complicated.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>